keywords:
  - Cell Painting
  - high-content screening
  - concentration-response
  - principal curves
  - UMAP
  - DBSCAN
  - R package
  - Shiny
abstract: >-
  cellpacman models the graded morphological response of each known compound
  in a Cell Painting screen as a concentration trajectory - a principal curve
  through the concentration-ordered DBSCAN centroids of that compound in a UMAP
  embedding of within-plate standardized features - and interprets unannotated
  compounds by projecting them onto these trajectories, which yields the most
  similar known compound and an interpolated effective concentration.
  Rank-based feature selection explains which morphological features drive a
  trajectory or separate two groups of wells. A companion Shiny application
  exposes the workflow interactively.
